groups.definition:
{{construction}} The individuals are a three-element set $X$ and $G$ is the group of all its permutations, which are arrows in every member. An intension $D$ of a relational type is admitted exactly when it is symmetric: $\langle\bar x,h\rangle\in D$ implies $\langle g\cdot\bar x,g\circ h\rangle\in D$ for every $g\in G$, where $g$ acts on individuals as itself and on entities of higher types by relabelling (Dorr's Definition 17). At a collapse $c_i$, the constant map with value $i$, it says that $D(c_{g(i)})=g\cdot D(c_i)$. Every such domain is finite and closed under the operations of C: each $g$ is an automorphism of the whole structure, and every parameter transported along a collapse is fixed by $G$, so the extension at a collapse of any definable intension is invariant. Evaluation point: the sole object, at the identity arrow.

